        SPECIFICITY","molecule":"Isoform 3"},{"texts":[{"value":"Composed of three
        domains: a modulating N-terminal domain, a DNA-binding domain and a C-terminal
        ligand-binding domain. The modulating domain, also known as A/B or AF-1 domain
        has a ligand-independent transactivation function. The C-terminus contains
        a ligand-dependent transactivation domain, also known as E/F or AF-2 domain
        which overlaps with the ligand binding domain. AF-1 and AF-2 activate transcription
        independently and synergistically and act in a promoter- and cell-specific
        manner. AF-1 seems to provide the major transactivation function in differentiated
        cells"}],"commentType":"DOMAIN"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10428798"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"14764652"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19339517"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21602804"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"7476978"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"7539106"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"7838153"}],"value":"Phosphorylated
        by cyclin A/CDK2 and CK1. Phosphorylation probably enhances transcriptional
        activity. Self-association induces phosphorylation. Dephosphorylation at Ser-118
        by PPP5C inhibits its transactivation activity. Phosphorylated by LMTK3 in
        vitro"}],"commentType":"PTM"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8999954"}],"value":"Glycosylated;
        contains N-acetylglucosamine, probably O-linked"}],"commentType":"PTM"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"P06211"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19383985"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21602804"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"28068668"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"37478846"}],"value":"Ubiquitinated;
        regulated by LATS1 via DCAF1 it leads to ESR1 proteasomal degradation (PubMed:21602804,
        PubMed:28068668). Deubiquitinated by OTUB1 (PubMed:19383985). Ubiquitinated
        by STUB1/CHIP; in the CA1 hippocampal region following loss of endogenous
        circulating estradiol (17-beta-estradiol/E2) (By similarity). Ubiquitinated
        by UBR5, leading to its degradation: UBR5 specifically recognizes and binds
        ligand-bound ESR1 when it is not associated with coactivators (NCOAs) (PubMed:37478846).
        In presence of NCOAs, the UBR5-degron is not accessible, preventing its ubiquitination
        and degradation (PubMed:37478846)"}],"commentType":"PTM"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"18657504"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"24498420"}],"value":"Dimethylated
        by PRMT1 at Arg-260. The methylation may favor cytoplasmic localization (PubMed:18657504,
        PubMed:24498420). Demethylated by JMJD6 at Arg-260 (PubMed:24498420)"}],"commentType":"PTM"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"22031296"}],"value":"Palmitoylated
        (isoform 3). Not biotinylated (isoform 3)"}],"commentType":"PTM"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"22031296"}],"value":"Palmitoylated
        by ZDHHC7 and ZDHHC21. Palmitoylation is required for plasma membrane targeting
        and for rapid intracellular signaling via ERK and AKT kinases and cAMP generation,
        but not for signaling mediated by the nuclear hormone receptor"}],"commentType":"PTM"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10942433"}],"value":"Genetic
        variations in ESR1 are correlated with bone mineral density (BMD). Low BMD
        is a risk factor for osteoporotic fracture. Osteoporosis is characterized
        by reduced bone mineral density, disruption of bone microarchitecture, and
        the alteration of the amount and variety of non-collagenous proteins in bone.
        Osteoporotic bones are more at risk of fracture"}],"commentType":"POLYMORPHISM"},{"commentType":"DISEASE","disease":{"diseaseId":"Estrogen
        resistance","diseaseAccession":"DI-03869","acronym":"ESTRR","description":"A
        disorder characterized by partial or complete resistance to estrogens, in
        the presence of elevated estrogen serum levels. Clinical features include
        absence of the pubertal growth spurt, delayed bone maturation, unfused epiphyses,
        reduced bone mineral density, osteoporosis, continued growth into adulthood
        and very tall adult stature. Glucose intolerance, hyperinsulinemia and lipid
        abnormalities may also be present.","diseaseCrossReference":{"database":"MIM","id":"615363"},"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"23841731"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"27754803"}]},"note":{"texts":[{"value":"The
        disease is caused by variants affecting the gene represented in this entry"}]}},{"texts":[{"value":"Selective
        estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs), such as tamoxifen, raloxifene, toremifene,
